From d14e4f0749b02d4a41f036e49f3f9955e7e5731f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Nick <nickjb78@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 24 Apr 2026 19:49:42 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] fix(input): deliver text-input-v3 enter/leave even when no
 IME present
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

CInputMethodRelay::onKeyboardFocus gates the entire body on
`!m_inputMethod.expired()`, but the body's only job is to dispatch
zwp_text_input_v3.enter / .leave to clients on keyboard focus changes,
which has nothing to do with whether an IME (fcitx, ibus, etc.) is
connected to the seat.

Symptom on a clean install with no IME running and an Electron app
launched with `--enable-wayland-ime --wayland-text-input-version=3`
(e.g. Claude Desktop, VS Code Insiders, Signal): the first time the
window receives keyboard focus typing works, but after ANY focus
change off-and-back (Alt+Tab, taskbar click, focuswindow dispatch,
workspace cycle) typing dies silently. wl_keyboard.enter still
arrives, uprobe-confirmed, but Electron's Wayland IME backend
latches into "not focused for text" state and ignores the keys until
something resyncs it (a real mouse click on the window goes through
processMouseDownNormal → refocus, which takes a different path).

Live verified via uprobe on the running compositor: pre-patch,
hypr_imr_kbd_focus fires on every dispatch but hypr_ti_enter never
fires; post-patch (NOP'd guard via gdb on the running PID, persisted
to disk via this commit), hypr_ti_enter / hypr_ti_leave fire on every
focus transition that involves a text-input-v3 client.

The two downstream paths that *do* care about IME presence, 
deactivateIME (called from CTextInput::leave) and activateIME (called
when the client enables text input), already check
m_inputMethod.expired() themselves, so the no-IME case stays correct
end-to-end.

This is upstream-Hyprland code (vaxerski 2022-08-05); will be filed
as a discussion / PR after a few sessions of bare-metal stability.

Co-Authored-By: singularity <singularity@entersingularity.ai>
---
 src/managers/input/InputMethodRelay.cpp | 26 ++++++++++++++++++++++---
 1 file changed, 23 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/managers/input/InputMethodRelay.cpp b/src/managers/input/InputMethodRelay.cpp
index 27fd80b6..a5f30ee0 100644
--- a/src/managers/input/InputMethodRelay.cpp
+++ b/src/managers/input/InputMethodRelay.cpp
@@ -131,9 +131,29 @@ void CInputMethodRelay::commitIMEState(CTextInput* pInput) {
 }
 
 void CInputMethodRelay::onKeyboardFocus(SP<CWLSurfaceResource> pSurface) {
-    if (m_inputMethod.expired())
-        return;
-
+    // Singularity patch: do NOT gate text-input-v3 enter/leave on
+    // having an IME registered. Upstream's `if (m_inputMethod.expired())
+    // return;` guard at the top of this function silently drops
+    // zwp_text_input_v3.enter delivery on every focus change when no
+    // IME (fcitx, ibus, etc.) is connected to the seat, but
+    // text-input-v3 is *also* the protocol that browsers and Electron
+    // apps with `--enable-wayland-ime --wayland-text-input-version=3`
+    // use to receive input in their built-in Wayland IME path. Without
+    // the enter event, those clients latch into "I'm not focused for
+    // text" state after the first focus change and typing dies until
+    // a real-mouse-click resyncs them via processMouseDownNormal →
+    // refocus.
+    //
+    // The wire-level enter/leave calls below have no IME dependency, 
+    // they're plain `zwp_text_input_v3.enter(surface)` / `.leave()`
+    // protocol events. CTextInput::leave() does call deactivateIME()
+    // at the end, but deactivateIME has its own m_inputMethod-expired
+    // gate, so the no-IME case is already handled correctly.
+    //
+    // Bug verified live via uprobe on a clean Singularity install
+    // running Claude Desktop (Electron, --wayland-text-input-version=3,
+    // no IME): hypr_imr_kbd_focus fires, hypr_kbd_enter_entry fires,
+    // hypr_ti_enter never fires. After this patch, ti_enter fires too.
     if (pSurface == m_lastKbFocus)
         return;
 
